---
title: Release Lifecycle (Release Train)
config:
    layout: elk
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB

    [*] --> accumulating: new train opens (on cadence, or after previous release closes)
    [*] --> accumulating: new patch train opens (from process inner-loop — hotfix merged)
    [*] --> accumulating: new patch train opens (from process backport — backport merged)

    accumulating --> cut: cadence timer elapses (time)
    accumulating --> cut: RM triggers cut script

    cut --> preparing: RM claims train

    preparing --> ready_for_release_decision: RM publishes release notes, tags release candidate

    ready_for_release_decision --> reviewing_release: PO claims train
    reviewing_release --> gated_nogo: PO defers with blocking reason
    reviewing_release --> deploying: PO approves go (or IC approves for patch train)

    gated_nogo --> reviewing_release: PO re-claims deferred train
    gated_nogo --> abandoning: PO claims to abandon train
    abandoning --> abandoned: PO closes train, issues revert to inner-loop.staged for next train

    deploying --> rolling_out: production deploy completes (external — progressive rollout starts)
    rolling_out --> ready_for_monitoring: rollout reaches 100% (from process progressive-rollout)

    ready_for_monitoring --> monitoring: on-call claims post-deploy watch

    monitoring --> released: on-call confirms post-deploy window clean
    monitoring --> rolling_back: on-call triggers rollback

    rolling_back --> rolled_back: rollback completes

    released --> [*]: terminal (shipped) — closes member issues via automation (experiments excluded)
    rolled_back --> [*]: terminal (reverted) — issues on train tagged reverted, regression bug filed
    abandoned --> [*]: terminal (abandoned) — issues released on next train

    note right of accumulating
        Resting. The train is open; every merge to the target branch that lands on inner-loop.staged is added to this train. Train membership is recorded at merge time.

        Three train variants share this state machine:
        • Standard train — opens on cadence or after previous release closes; target branch is main; PO approves.
        • Hotfix patch train — spawned by IC during an incident; target branch is main; single issue (the hotfix); IC bypasses the release gate and approves directly.
        • Backport patch train — spawned by backport lifecycle; target branch is an older release branch; single issue (the backport); IC approves.

        Cross-process legend:
        • Entry events: cadence timer (internal), previous release terminal (internal), from process inner-loop (hotfix merged spawns patch train), from process backport (backport merged spawns patch train). All entries are spawn events — the train is a new work item, not a shared state.
        • Exit external: rolling_out hands off to process progressive-rollout (which ramps the flag 1% → 100%); rolling_out re-enters from progressive-rollout when complete.
        • Exit terminal: released closes member issues via automation. Experiment-type issues (type:exp) are EXCLUDED from auto-closure — they spawn into experimentation and close at verdict on that lifecycle. Rolled_back / abandoned are internal terminals.
    end note

    note right of cut
        Resting. Train is closed to new merges. Any merge arriving after cut joins the NEXT train. Cut is the point of no return for this train's contents.
    end note

    note right of preparing
        Working. RM is compiling release notes (from merged PR descriptions) and tagging the release candidate (v{major}.{minor}.{patch}).

        RM (Release Manager) owns release mechanics. In small orgs, a PM, tech lead, or on-call engineer wears the RM hat. In larger orgs, a dedicated RM or Release Engineering team owns this. The diagram uses the role, not the person — map to whoever in your org carries that accountability.
    end note

    note right of ready_for_release_decision
        Queue for PO. Claim SLA matches release cadence.
    end note

    note right of reviewing_release
        Working. PO evaluates: is staging green, do release notes match expectations, any known blockers, any external coordination required?

        For hotfix and backport patch trains, the IC is the approver (scoped to the incident). The IC bypasses release-cadence concerns — if the patch is stable and staging is green, go. This authority is documented in the incident-response process.
    end note

    note right of gated_nogo
        Resting. PO deferred with a documented blocking reason ("waiting for fix #456," "defer until Thursday demo"). Vague no-gos indicate undefined release criteria.
        PO can re-claim when blockers clear, or claim to abandon if deferral is indefinite.
    end note

    note right of deploying
        System-working (resting). CI promotes the RC tag to production. Typically short. Failure here transitions to rolling_back via on-call.
    end note

    note right of rolling_out
        Resting from the release's perspective. The progressive-rollout state machine is doing the work of ramping the flag from 1% → 100%. A kill_switched rollout returns here for rollback.
    end note

    note right of monitoring
        Working. On-call developer watches error rates, latency, alerts for 30 min (standard) or 60 min (major/hotfix) post-100%.
    end note

    note right of released
        Terminal (shipped). Release automation closes each member issue (this is the actual close event — PRs use "Refs #N", not "Closes #N", so the issue stayed open from merge through rollout until this moment). Experiment-type issues (type:exp) are excluded from auto-closure — they close at verdict on the experimentation lifecycle. Each closed issue is marked released.
    end note

    note right of rolled_back
        Terminal (reverted). Rollback branches off monitoring, so issues on the train never reached released and were NOT auto-closed — they remain open, tagged reverted. A compensating regression bug is filed as a new raw issue; the reverted issues re-enter refinement for re-work or cancellation.
    end note

    note right of abandoned
        Terminal (abandoned). Train never deployed. Issues flow back into inner-loop.staged to be picked up by the next train.
    end note
